This code will display the value "value if condition is true" if the condition is true, and "value if condition is false" if the condition is false. The `\ifnum` command is used to compare the value of the counter `\value{counter}` to the specified value. If the condition is true, the code between `\ifnum` and `\else` is executed; otherwise, the code between `\else` and `\fi` is executed.

For example, if you want to display "Even" if the value of the counter `counter` is even and "Odd" if it is odd, you can use:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\newcounter{counter}
\begin{document}

\setcounter{counter}{5}

|\\( \text{if} \quad \text{condition} \quad \text{then} \quad \text{value if condition is true} \quad \text{else} \quad \text{value if condition is false} \\
\end{array} \\
\end{document}
```

This code will display the value "value if condition is true" if the condition is true, and "value if condition is false" if the condition is false. The `\if` command is a conditional command in LaTeX that allows you to specify different content based on the evaluation of a condition.

For example, if you want to display "The number is even" if a number is even and "The number is odd" if a number is odd, you can use the following code:

```latex
